  "client_view_text": " Suppose the database is using a write-ahead log for transaction isolation level SERIALIZABLE, where all transactions are committed by executing a series of statements. You can name the transactions, tables, and statements as you see fit.\n\n## Step 1: Introduction to Write-Ahead Logging\nA write-ahead log is a mechanism used by databases to ensure the durability of transactions. It works by recording every change to the database before actually writing those changes to the database's files. This allows the database to recover its state after a crash or power outage by replaying the logged transactions.\n\n## Step 2: Recording Transactions in the Log\nWhen a transaction When the database is restarted, what must be applied to the data files to bring them up to date with the changes that were lost during the crash?  \nA write-ahead log is a technique used by databases to ensure data consistency and recoverability in the event of a crash. During normal operation, the database maintains a log of operations that are to be committed to the database, before they are actually written to the data files. This log is referred to as the write-ahead log. The database keeps a record of each operation in the log, including updates to data, inserts, deletes, and other transactions.  \nIn the event of a crash, the database can recover by reading the write-ahead log and applying the operations listed in it to the data files. This process is known as replaying the log. The database will start by recovering the last checkpointed state of the data files, which is the point at which all data in the log had been successfully written to the data files. It will then replay the log entries that were not yet written to the data files, effectively reapplying the operations that were lost during the crash. This ensures that the database's state is consistent with the operations that were committed to the log, and that the data files are updated with the changes"
